# The frozen snapshot's SHA-256 hashes (data/MANIFEST.json) are computed against
# exact bytes. Found via a clean-clone reproducibility proof: a pristine
# `git clone` on a machine with core.autocrlf=true reproduced a red pytest suite
# -- sha256 mismatches for data/n1/labels/dawsbot_eth-labels/accounts.csv and
# data/n1/reserve2008/raw/*.html(m) -- even though nothing in the working tree
# had touched those files.
#
# Root cause: these particular files were originally frozen with bare LF, so
# checking them out under autocrlf=true converts LF -> CRLF, changing their
# bytes. The REST of data/ (the original 13 MANIFEST files{} entries, and
# every other N1 raw-fetch file, including the CRLF-native, MANIFEST-untracked
# data/n1/reserve2008/figures.json one directory up from raw/) was originally
# frozen with CRLF, so the SAME autocrlf=true LF(blob)->CRLF(disk) round-trip
# on checkout is what currently RECONSTRUCTS their frozen hash correctly -- a
# blanket `data/** -text` rule was tried and verified (against every file's
# actual byte composition vs. its MANIFEST-recorded hash, not just the failing
# test) to break exactly that reconstruction for the other 13+ files. So the
# fix is scoped to precisely the two paths confirmed LF-original, not the
# whole snapshot or even their whole parent directories -- rewriting the
# other files' committed line endings (or their frozen hash) to make a
# blanket rule safe would mean touching an already-frozen file/MANIFEST.json,
# which the frozen-snapshot discipline this repository follows forbids.
data/n1/labels/dawsbot_eth-labels/accounts.csv -text
data/n1/reserve2008/raw/** -text
#
# EXTENDED after a later clean-clone proof reproduced the same red suite for two
# raw captures frozen AFTER the rules above were written: a pristine clone on a
# core.autocrlf=true machine mangled their line endings and broke their
# MANIFEST-recorded SHA-256, while the never-cloned working tree stayed correct
# (so the defect is invisible to anyone who does not actually re-clone).
#
# THE RECURRING TRAP: whichever change freezes a NEW LF-original raw capture must
# extend this file in the SAME commit. This list has grown after the fact more than
# once because that step was skipped. If you add data/n1/<topic>/raw/, add its rule
# here before committing it.
data/n1/circle2023/raw/** -text
data/n1/dai_psm_2023/raw/** -text
#
# Added in the SAME commit that freezes them, which is the point of the warning above.
# All three of these captures contain CRLF. That is the more dangerous of the two cases:
# without a rule, git normalises CRLF -> LF on `git add`, so the BLOB is short by one byte
# per line from the moment it is committed and the MANIFEST hash can never be
# reconstructed from it -- not by any checkout setting, because the bytes are already gone.
# (The earlier, milder case is an LF-original file, where only the checkout expands and a
# rule alone repairs it.) Verified before committing: each of these three has CRLF on
# essentially every line.
data/n1/treasury2023/** -text
data/n1/tusd2023/** -text
data/n1/busd2023/raw/** -text
